  "query_preview": "..\n\nMid-level clouds from this morning are dissipating while high\nclouds are moving in from the west and low-level cumulus clouds\nare forming along high terrain to the east. A few isolated\nthunderstorms are developing closer to the International Border.\nMoisture continues to surge into the area with PWATs Tucson \nwestward now 1.5 to 1.7 inches and 1.2 to 1.5 inches for areas \neastward. Southwest flow over southern Arizona will shift to be \nmore from the south tonight as the trough pushes eastward and \nhigh pressure over New Mexico will oscillate westward. Current \nCAMs show slightly more storms and showers this afternoon than \nyesterday with a chance for activity tonight stemming due to \noutflows extending northward from Sonora, MX. Storms will be \nfocused to areas along the International Border and along the \nAZ/NM border. Tomorrow looks to have a similar set up as today.\n\nHigh pressure moving in from the east will continue this week\nnearly centering over southern Arizona on Wednesday and \nThursday. Coverage in storms looks to increase gradually this \nwork week, with the best instability and forcing in the later \nhalf of the week. As always with the monsoon, it's hard to see \nwhich day will be the most active out of the bunch, but details \nwill become clearer as we get closer. With the available \nmoisture and weak flow aloft, the primary threat continues to be\nthe flash flooding potential from efficient heavy rainers, with\nstrong outflow winds as a secondary threat.\n\nTemperatures will be within a couple of degrees of normal through \nWednesday, then dropping to below normal levels late next week.",
